WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
there's almost a countless number of  easter eggs scattered across the movies  of the mcu  some are subtle while others are far  more obvious and although it's almost  impossible to list them all these are a  few that you might have just missed  for years on end the worlds of the  avengers and the x-men have been kept  separate on the big screen thanks to a  conflict in usage rights between various  studios  while the avengers assembled at marvel  studios the mutants had their own  adventures over at fox but that doesn't  mean there haven't been any knowing  winks exchanged between the two  during one scene in iron man tony stark  conducts a test flight of his new armor  for the first time and decides to see  just how high he can fly  he asks his ai assistant jarvis what the  altitude record is for a very specific  plane  all right let's see what this thing can  do what's sr-71's record the altitude  record for fixed-wing flight is 85 000  feet  that's a real plane of course but it  also has a place in the marvel universe  the sr-71 blackbird was used as the  model for the jet flown to missions by  the x-men  and has often been referred to as the  blackbird whenever it's not being called  the x-jet  now iron man can't exactly specify that  stark was referring to charles xavier's  private super plane but it was a nice  nod in the direction of x-men fans  nonetheless  captain america the first avenger takes  marvel fans back to world war ii  not only the era in which steve rogers  became captain america  but also the time during which the  character was created in real life  but he wasn't the only superhero to come  out of this period during the stark  expo scene eagle-eyed viewers will be  able to spot another classic marvel  character who was created back when the  company was still known as timely comics  as steve bucky and their dates walk  through the expo  they pass a glass display case with a  costume in it labeled dr phineus horton  presents the synthetic man  horton is the scientist who created the  original human torch all the way back in  marvel comics number one  this character was initially kept in a  glass cylinder much like the one in  captain america  remains an important piece of the  foundations of the marvel universe  this particular shot establishes that  some version of him existed in the mcu  at some point so  maybe one day we'll flash back again and  get the full story of synthetic man and  his adventures  the marvel cinematic universe may be a  world unto itself but it exists in the  greater realm of marvel entertainment  which itself contains a vast multiverse  of various worlds featuring many  alternate versions of its characters  the mcu even has its own designated  reality number  19999 this places it within the greater  marvel universe meaning that  whether we'll ever see it or not there's  a cosmic link between the cinematic  versions of these characters and their  prime continuity comic book counterparts  that link was further emphasized in thor  the dark world  when eric selvig laid out his  understanding of reality on a giant  chalkboard during his time in a mental  institution  among his scribblings are the words 616  universe  the official designation of the main  marvel comics universe  any questions  yeah can i have my shoe back although  stan lee's cameos and marvel movies have  become  practically legendary lee isn't the only  marvel luminary to pop up in the mcu at  one point or another  one of the most notable instances of his  fame came during captain america the  winter soldier  as alexander pierce is questioning the  winter soldier a team of handlers are  standing around the room  one of them seen on the left is ed  brubacher an eisner award-winning comic  book writer responsible for resurrecting  bucky barnes as the winter soldier in  captain america number  one back in 2005. before brubaker joined  captain america bucky had been dead for  decades and was long considered one of  the rare comics characters who would  never return from the grave  without brubacher the mcu would be a  very different place indeed so  it's nice to see him get a little moment  of recognition here  no easter egg within the entirety of the  mcu is quite as bountiful as the  collector's hideaway on nowhere in  guardians of the galaxy  his massive treasure trove of artifacts  and living specimens seems to go on  forever  and if you're willing to be a little  liberal with the pause button you're  likely to notice quite a few  interesting things very well then  let us see what you brought among his  items are a chitari warrior from the  avengers a dark elf from  thor the dark world cosmo the space dog  and of course  powered the duck however there's one  more find tucked away in that room that  could have  wide reaching implications for the  future of the mcu  it's possible to spot a certain  cocoon-like object that writer-director  james gunn later hinted was the  gestation place of adam warlock a  character further teased in a  post-credits scene from volume 2.  as it turns out the two objects might  not be the exact same cocoon but the  visual cues are difficult to ignore  adam warlock has massive cosmic  implications for marvel's future so if  he does show up at some point  we can probably tie it all back to this  some easter eggs are so obscure that  you'll only recognize  them if you carefully read one very  specific comic book  that's the case here with this gem from  captain america civil war  at peggy carter's funeral her niece  sharon delivers the eulogy  and passes on some words of wisdom her  aunt once shared with her  even if the whole world is telling you  to move  it is your duty to plant yourself like a  tree  look them in the eye and say no it's a  powerful moment for certain comic book  readers however it was made all the more  powerful by the fact that it's a speech  lifted largely from amazing spider-man  number 537  a comic tying in to the original civil  war event in which a  very similar speech is given by none  other than captain america himself  screenwriters christopher marcus and  steven mcfeely definitely did their  homework with this one  some easter eggs may begin as an amusing  idea but sadly just don't come together  and fail to ever reach a satisfying  payoff  in the mcu where some parts of the  universe are attempting to work with the  same concepts as others  this can happen a lot for example it was  the case with one particular hidden gem  in doctor strange which has been  semi-retconned by the events of hulu's  runaways tv series  at various points in the film we see  glimpses of the hong kong sanctum where  sorcerers work to guard that particular  corner of the world from a host of  magical threats  among them is a staff-wielding character  named tina minoru  played in doctor strange by linda louise  duan we only know her name because she's  listed in the credits but minoru happens  to be the  mother of nico minoru one of the main  stars of runaways  who eventually wields her mother's staff  herself of course tina minoru was recast  for runaways but  it's still a nice touch some marvel  characters might not be ready for prime  time yet but are still loved by enough  fans and filmmakers to make at least a  brief appearance in the mcu  one instance of this happening occurs  during thor ragnarok when we see the  grand masters palace on sakhar for the  first time  this towering structure is adorned with  various shimmering statues depicting the  heads of champions who have fought in  the arena  and while the hulk head under  construction might end up dominating the  whole thing the heads that have already  been built are particularly fascinating  for comic book fans  they include bye beast man thing ares  and even beta ray bill a long time ally  of thor who fans have been hoping to see  on the big screen for years  we may not see any of these characters  actually living and breathing in the mcu  anytime  soon but these statues were enough to  let us know they exist  and that's a good start avengers  infinity war is very loosely based on  the 1991 marvel comics event series the  infinity gauntlet  while the plot of the movie differs  significantly from its origin story the  film  bears many nods and links to the text  for example bruce banner crashes through  the roof of dr strange's sanctum echoing  the moment when the silver surfer did  the same thing in the comics  i understood that reference of all the  moments that made gauntlet fans happy  however  one that really stands out comes in the  film's very last shot when  thanos sits down at his humble farm to  watch the sun rise on a grateful  universe  as he walks through the hut you'll see  something standing amid the crops  growing  outside a scarecrow made from his old  battle armor  at the end of infinity gauntlet thanos  gives up his quest for ultimate power  after the battle  takes up farming and makes a scarecrow  with his armor  these may be two very different ways of  arriving at the same image but it's a  great reference nonetheless

